045417] [17592186252951 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252951 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252951 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186252266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252951 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "52cd9670-efaa-5997-a26c-de39051a6431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253176 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253176 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253176 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186251091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253176 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da13ec6c-83c7-50f9-876b-4ab3d2ef21e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253490 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=720y-8{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=5\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edf79f32-6c49-50fc-bc9d-59df10630ea5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253490 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8y(90-y),\\,y(0)=5.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=90.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $90.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253611 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253611 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253611 #edb/ref 1071 "The population $y(t)$ of giraffes on a certain African wildlife reserve follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=17.2y-0.004{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=180,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of giraffes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253611 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "819cfa7f-e175-52e2-b6a4-00c7d6cd6241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253611 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =17.2y-0.004{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.004y(4\\,300-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=4\\,300.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $4\\,300,$ and we conclude that the number of giraffes cannot exceed $4\\,300.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319789 #edb/ref 1080 "4000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319789 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319789 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8351bd80-e518-5ece-9f96-abee1eb4c668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320179 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320179 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320179 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186319789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320179 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0b990bb7-090c-58a0-b6cc-f6b6c5614b8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321135 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321135 #edb/ref 1069 "q-353483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321135 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321135 #edb/ref 1071 "The download count $P(t)$ of a viral mobile game follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4P(8\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=600.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the download count growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "542b4bd1-a5e6-5564-a6fe-12f10ea4329d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321135 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/90/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFrom our equation, we see that $a=8000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $8000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8000=4000.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330089 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\,000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330089 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330089 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "101ea620-7c24-59db-9258-511ec049dc5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330271 #edb/ref 1080 "500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330271 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330271 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "255d3436-78af-5f95-8551-acbf5c7be1fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330804 #edb/ref 1080 "800"